The Debian build system is fundamentally different from Yocto. Here's how it works:
Binary-based approach:

- Debian maintains pre-compiled binary packages (.deb files) in repositories
- Tools like apt, dpkg, and debootstrap install these binaries
- rpi-image-gen uses these precompiled packages from Debian and Raspberry Pi OS repositories rather than compiling from source
- Packages are built once by Debian maintainers, then distributed

Package building (when you DO compile):

- Uses dpkg-buildpackage, debhelper, and debian/rules files
- Each package has a debian/ directory with build instructions
- Source packages follow strict Debian policy
- Results in .deb files uploaded to repositories

Image creation tools:

debootstrap - Creates minimal Debian root filesystem
live-build - Creates live/installable images
rpi-image-gen - Raspberry Pi's modern tool built on these foundations

//--------------------//
//--------------------//
//--------------------//

Yocto Project:

Source-based - Compiles everything from scratch (kernel, packages, toolchain)
Highly customizable - Control every compile flag, patch, dependency
Build time - Hours to build a complete image
Use case - When you need extreme customization, specific kernel versions, or non-standard architectures

Debian approach (rpi-image-gen):

Binary-based - Downloads pre-built packages
Less flexible - Limited to what's in repositories
Build time - Minutes to assemble an image
Use case - Standard hardware, faster iteration, easier maintenance

Analogy:

Yocto = Building a car from raw materials in a factory
Debian/rpi-image-gen = Assembling a car from pre-manufactured parts

If you're targeting standard Raspberry Pi hardware and can work with Debian/Raspberry Pi OS packages, rpi-image-gen is much faster and simpler. Use Yocto only if you need:

Custom kernel configurations
Ultra-minimal images (< 50MB)
Non-standard hardware support
Complete control over every compiled binary

