//--------------------//


binfmt_misc is a Linux kernel feature. On Windows with Docker Desktop:

Docker Desktop runs a Linux VM (WSL2 or Hyper-V)
That Linux VM has its own kernel
The QEMU registration happens inside that VM's kernel

//--------------------//
//--------------------//
//--------------------//

Windows Host (Jenkins)
    ↓
Docker Desktop (Linux VM with kernel)
    ↓
Step 1: docker run multiarch/qemu-user-static
        → Registers QEMU in the VM's kernel
        → Copies QEMU binaries into the VM
    ↓
Step 2: docker run debian:bookworm (your build container)
        → This container runs in the SAME VM
        → Shares the SAME kernel with binfmt_misc
        → Can now execute ARM binaries automatically
    ↓
Your rpi-image-gen build runs ARM tools
        → Kernel sees ARM binary
        → Automatically runs it through QEMU
        → Works transparently


Both containers (the QEMU registration one and your Debian build one) run in the same Linux VM, sharing the same kernel. So the binfmt_misc registration persists.

//--------------------//
//--------------------//
//--------------------//

Simplified explanation:

1. First container registers: "Hey kernel, when you see ARM binaries, use QEMU"
2. First container exits (with --rm)
3. Your Debian container starts
4. When it tries to run ARM code, the kernel (which is still aware of the registration) automatically uses QEMU
It just works™

The -p yes flag is crucial here - it makes the QEMU binaries persistent in the Docker VM, so they're still available after the registration container exits.

